From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 10 Jul 2026 16:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] pipeline: simple: Raise default stream buffer count to 8

The processed-stream bufferCount defaults to 4. Through the PipeWire
libcamera SPA source the negotiated buffer ring is pinned to exactly
this value (spa libcamera-source advertises
SPA_POD_CHOICE_RANGE_Int(n, n, n) with n = streamConfig.bufferCount).

A deep consumer pipeline holds several of those buffers concurrently.
GNOME Snapshot's camerabin chain (decodebin3/multiqueue + tee + two
queues + the GTK4 paintable which keeps the current and pending frame
alive) retains 5+ buffers, exhausting a 4-buffer ring. Measured on
meizu-m1721 (msm8953, SoftISP): the viewfinder then cycles between
~0.4 s bursts at the sensor rate and ~1.05 s windows where the source
delivers nothing (frames dropped at the producer), i.e. ~9 fps
average with metronomic 1 s freezes, while a shallow
pipewiresrc ! fakesink pipeline on the same node sustains a steady
28.6 fps.

Raise the default to 8 so a deep client pipeline leaves buffers
cycling. Requests in flight to the capture device remain capped by
kMaxQueuedRequestsDevice (4) and the bayer-side kNumInternalBuffers
is unchanged, so device behaviour is not affected; the cost is four
more output buffers from the dma-buf heap (~8.3 MB each at 1080p
BGRx).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
diff --git a/src/libcamera/pipeline/simple/simple.cpp b/src/libcamera/pipeline/simple/simple.cpp
--- a/src/libcamera/pipeline/simple/simple.cpp
+++ b/src/libcamera/pipeline/simple/simple.cpp
@@ -389,7 +389,14 @@
 	const Transform &combinedTransform() const { return combinedTransform_; }

 private:
-	static constexpr unsigned int kNumBuffersDefault = 4;
+	/*
+	 * Deep consumer pipelines (PipeWire SPA ring == bufferCount; e.g.
+	 * GNOME Snapshot's camerabin holds 5+ buffers across multiqueue,
+	 * tee, queues and the GTK paintable) starve a 4-buffer ring and the
+	 * preview stalls in ~1 s cycles. 8 leaves headroom; device-side
+	 * queue depth stays capped by kMaxQueuedRequestsDevice.
+	 */
+	static constexpr unsigned int kNumBuffersDefault = 8;
 	static constexpr unsigned int kNumBuffersMax = 32;

 	/*
